"GitHub - gonality/discrete-vs-metric-dgon: Code and figures to accompany the paper \"Discrete and metric divisorial gonality can be different\" · GitHub (https://github.com/gonality/discrete-vs-metric-dgon)\nciteturn3search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; For gonality computations, we use the programs `find_gonality`, `subdivision_conjecture` and `Brill_Noether_geng` from dgon-tools. ... To verify that each of these is a counterexample, run ... Discrete and metric divisorial gonality can be different, preprint, 2021. https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.12568\nmore_cycles_on_the_outer_ring.in  | more_cycles_on_the_outer_ring.in  |    |  \nmore_cycles_on_the_outer_ring.pdf  | more_cycles_on_the_outer_ring.pdf  |    |  \nmore_cycles_on_the_outer_ring.tex  | more_cycles_on_the_outer_ring.tex  |    |  \n\n## Repository files navigation\n\n# Code and figures to accompany the paper \"Discrete and metric divisorial gonality can be different\"\n\nThis repository contains additional material accompanying the paper [DSW21], including more figures and instructions for reproducing our computational results. Contents:\n\n  0. Links to the relevant source code;\n  1. Instructions to verify the counterexamples from [DSW21, Figure 4];\n  2. Drawings of several examples with more than 3 cycles on the outer ring, and instructions to compute the corresponding gonalities;\n  3. Drawings of all 29 simple counterexamples on 10 vertices, and instructions to reproduce this list;\n  4. Instructions to verify the Brill–Noether conjecture for all simple graphs on at most 13 vertices.\n\n## 0. Where to find the code\n\nFor gonality computations, we use the programs `find_gonality`, `subdivision_conjecture` and `Brill_Noether_geng` from dgon-tools. To generate graphs, we sometimes use the program `geng` from the `gtools` suite packaged with `nauty`; see [MP20]. For instructions to compile these programs, please refer to the documentation of those projects.\n\nThe relevant input files (`Figure_4.in` and `more_cycles_on_the_outer_ring.in`) are part of this repository; see above.\n\n## 1. Verifying the examples from [DSW21, Figure 4]\n\nIn [DSW21, Figure 4], a total of 8 counterexamples are shown. To verify that each of these is a counterexample, run\n    \n    `subdivision_conjecture -vv < Figure_4.in\n    `\n\nThe vertex labels used in the input file `Figure_4.in` are depicted in the file `Figure_4.pdf`.\n\n## 2. More than 3 cycles on the outer ring\n\nWe also considered graphs where the outer ring consists of n cycles for some n ≥ 3. \n## 4. Testing the Brill–Noether conjecture\n\nWe tested the gonality conjecture dgon(G) ≤ ⌊(g+3)/2⌋ for all simple graphs G on at most 13 vertices. We found no counterexamples. This can be reproduced by running\n    \n    `Brill_Noether_geng -v 13\n    `\n\nWarning: this computation took approximately 8000 CPU hours. The program `Brill_Noether_geng` is capable of dividing the task into smaller subtasks. We divided this task into 48 subtasks, of which we ran 8 at a time on an Intel(R) Xeon(R) CPU E3-1240 V2 @ 3.40GHz. Each set of 8 simultaneous subtasks took about a week, so the entire computation lasted about 6 weeks. The exact command we used is the following:\n    \n    `Brill_Noether_geng -v 13 $i/48\n    `\n\nwhere `$i` is an integer in the range 0,...,47 denoting the number of the current subprocess.\n\n## References\n\n[DSW21]: Josse van Dobben de Bruyn, Harry Smit, and Marieke van der Wegen. Discrete and metric divisorial gonality can be different, preprint, 2021. https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.12568\n\n[MP20]: Brendan D. McKay and Adolfo Piperno, nauty and Traces, version 2.7r1, 2020. https://pallini.di.uniroma1.it.\n\n## About\n\nCode and figures to accompany the paper \"Discrete and metric divisorial gonality can be different\"\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDifference sets and tri-weight linear codes from trinomials over binary fields (https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.10042)\nciteturn3academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.2 years ago; In the course of confirming the conjecture, we show that these trinomials share the remarkable property that every element of the value-set of each trinomial has either one or four preimages. We also give a partial resolution of another conjecture of Cun Sheng Ding~\\cite{Ding-Discrete} claiming that linear codes constructed from those eleven trinomials are tri-weight.\nTitle: Difference sets and tri-weight linear codes from trinomials over binary fields\nAuthors: Omran Ahmadi, Masoud Shafaeiabr\nDate: Mon Aug 23 10:16:00 2021\n\nWe confirm a conjecture of Cun Sheng Ding~\\cite{Ding-Discrete} claiming that the punctured value-sets of a list of eleven trinomials over odd-degree extensions of the binary field give rise to difference sets with Singer parameters. In the course of confirming the conjecture, we show that these trinomials share the remarkable property that every element of the value-set of each trinomial has either one or four preimages. We also give a partial resolution of another conjecture of Cun Sheng Ding~\\cite{Ding-Discrete} claiming that linear codes constructed from those eleven trinomials are tri-weight.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDelft University of Technology (https://pure.tudelft.nl/ws/portalfiles/portal/116645495/1_s2.0_S0097316522000279_main.pdf)\nciteturn3search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; A 189 (2022) 105619 ... [7,6]). ... geometry (e.g. a combinatorial proof of the Brill–Noether theorem, [10]). ... remaining open problem is the Brill–Noether conjecture for ﬁnite graphs [3, Conj. ... divisorial gonality of a graph is equal to the divisorial gonality of the associated metric\n3.9(1)], \nbased on an analogous result for curves. We focus on the r = 1 case of this conjecture. \nLet dgonr(G) denote the smallest degree of a rank r divisor on G, and let dgon(G) :=\ndgon1(G) denote the divisorial gonality of G (see §2 for deﬁnitions). Then the r = 1 case \nof the Brill–Noether conjecture can be stated as follows.\nConjecture 1.1 (Gonality conjecture, [3, Conjecture 3.10(1)]). Let G be a connected loop-\nless multigraph, and let g := |E(G)| −|V (G)| + 1 denote its cyclomatic number. Then \ndgon(G) ≤⌊g+3\n2 ⌋.\nThe corresponding result for metric graphs was proven by Baker [3, Thm. 3.12] using \nalgebraic geometry. A purely combinatorial proof of this result was recently found by \nDraisma and Vargas [14], with many promising avenues still to be explored [15]. However, \nfor discrete graphs, Conjecture 1.1 is still wide open.1 Partial results were obtained by \nAtanasov and Ranganathan [2], who proved Conjecture 1.1 for all graphs of genus at \nmost 5, and by Aidun and Morrison [1], who proved the conjecture for Cartesian product \ngraphs.\nThe most straightforward approach to Conjecture 1.1 would be to show that the \ndivisorial gonality of a graph is equal to the divisorial gonality of the associated metric \ngraph with unit lengths. This is the second remaining conjecture of Baker’s paper [3, \nConj. 3.14]. Given a multigraph G and an integer k ≥1, let σk(G) denote the multigraph \nobtained from G by subdividing every edge into k parts. The conjecture can then be \nstated as follows.\nConjecture 1.2 ([3, Conjecture 3.14]). Let G be a connected loopless multigraph, let Γ(G)\nbe the corresponding metric graph with unit edge lengths, and let r ≥1. Then:\n1 A proof of Conjecture 1.1 (and more generally [3, Conj. 3.9(1)]) was given by Caporaso [9, Thm. 6.3], but \na gap in this proof was later pointed out by Sam Payne and reported by Baker and Jensen in [4, Rmk. 4.8 \nand footnote 5]. To our knowledge, this has not been repaired.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpecialization of linear systems from curves to graphs (https://msp.org/ant/2008/2-6/ant-v2-n6-p01-p.pdf)\nciteturn3search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.6 years ago; ** Every graph of genus 2 is hyperelliptic, since by the Riemann–Roch theorem for graphs, the canonical divisor \\(K_G\\) has degree 2 and dimension 1. ... ** Classically, it is known by *Brill–Noether theory* that every smooth curve of genus \\(g\\) over the complex numbers has gonality at most \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1}{2}(g+3)\\right\\rfloor\\), and this bound is tight: for every \\(g \\ge 0\\), the general curve of genus \\(g\\) has gonality exactly equal to \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1}{2}(g+3)\\right\\rfloor\\). ... Based on extensive computer calculations by Adam Tart (an undergraduate at Georgia Tech), we conjecture that similar results hold in the purely combinatorial setting of finite graphs:\nMore generally:\n\n**Theorem 3.8** (Classical Brill–Noether theory). *Fix integers \\(g, r, d \\ge 0\\), and define the Brill–Noether number*\n\\[\n\\rho(g,r,d)=g-(r+1)(g-d+r).\n\\]\n\n*Then*\n\n(1) *if \\(\\rho(g,r,d)\\ge 0\\), then every smooth curve \\(X/\\mathbb{C}\\) of genus \\(g\\) has a divisor \\(D\\) with \\(r(D)=r\\) and \\(\\deg(D)\\le d\\);*\n\n(2) *if \\(\\rho(g,r,d)<0\\), then on a general smooth curve of genus \\(g\\), there is no divisor \\(D\\) with \\(r(D)=r\\) and \\(\\deg(D)\\le d\\).*\n\nBased on extensive computer calculations by Adam Tart (an undergraduate at Georgia Tech), we conjecture that similar results hold in the purely combinatorial setting of finite graphs:\n\n**Conjecture 3.9** (Brill–Noether conjecture for graphs). *Fix integers \\(g, r, d \\ge 0\\), and set*\n\\[\n\\rho(g,r,d)=g-(r+1)(g-d+r).\n\\]\n\n*Then*\n\n(1) *if \\(\\rho(g,r,d)\\ge 0\\), then every graph of genus \\(g\\) has a divisor \\(D\\) with \\(r(D)=r\\) and \\(\\deg(D)\\le d\\);*\n\n(2) *if \\(\\rho(g,r,d)<0\\), there exists a graph of genus \\(g\\) for which there is no divisor \\(D\\) with \\(r(D)=r\\) and \\(\\deg(D)\\le d\\).*\n\nIn the special case \\(r=1\\), Conjecture 3.9 can be reformulated as follows:\n\n**Conjecture 3.10** (Gonality conjecture for graphs). *For each integer \\(g \\ge 0\\),*\n\n(1) *the gonality of any graph of genus \\(g\\) is at most \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1}{2}(g+3)\\right\\rfloor\\) and*--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Difference sets and tri-weight linear codes from trinomials over binary fields (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/354088064_Difference_sets_and_tri-weight_linear_codes_from_trinomials_over_binary_fields)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.2 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; In the course of confirming the conjecture, we show that these trinomials share the remarkable property that every element of the value-set of each trinomial has either one or four preimages. ... We also give a partial resolution of another conjecture of Cun Sheng Ding~\\cite{Ding-Discrete} claiming that linear codes constructed from those eleven trinomials are tri-weight. ... about tri-weight linear codes constructed from the above polynomials. ... We could not use the above observation towards a proof of Theorem 1.\nPreprint PDF Available\n\n# Difference sets and tri-weight linear codes from trinomials over binary fields\n\n  * August 2021\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2108.10042\n\n  * License\n  * CC BY 4.0\n\nAuthors:\n\nOmran Ahmadi Darvishvand\n\n  * Institute for Research in Fundamental Sciences\n\nMasoud Shafaeiabr\n\nMasoud Shafaeiabr\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage\n\nDownload file PDFRead file\n\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer reviewed yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe confirm a conjecture of Cun Sheng Ding~\\cite{Ding-Discrete} claiming that the punctured value-sets of a list of eleven trinomials over odd-degree extensions of the binary field give rise to difference sets with Singer parameters. In the course of confirming the conjecture, we show that these trinomials share the remarkable property that every element of the value-set of each trinomial has either one or four preimages. We also give a partial resolution of another conjecture of Cun Sheng Ding~\\cite{Ding-Discrete} claiming that linear codes constructed from those eleven trinomials are tri-weight.\n\nImage: ResearchGate Logo\n\nDiscover the world's research\n\n  * 25+ million members\n  * 160+ million publication pages\n  * 2.3+ billion citations\nJoin for free\n\nPublic Full-text 1\n\nAvailable via license: CC BY 4.0\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\narXiv:2108.10042v1 [math.CO] 23 Aug 2021\n\nDIFFERENCE SETS AND TRI-WEIGHT LINEAR CODES FROM\n\nTRINOMIALS OVER BINARY FIELDS\n\nOMRAN AHMADI AND MASOUD SHAFAEIABR\n\nAbstract. We conﬁrm a conjecture of Cun Sheng Ding [5] claiming that the punctured\n\nvalue-sets of a list of eleven trinomials over odd-degree extensions of the binary ﬁeld\n\ngive rise to diﬀerence sets with Singer parameters. In the course of conﬁrming the\n\nconjecture, we show that these trinomials share the remarkable property that every\n\nelement of the value-set of each trinomial has either one or four preiamges . We also\n\ngive partial resolution of another conjecture of Cun Sheng Ding [5] claiming that linear\n\ncodes constructed from those eleven trinomials are tri-weight.\n\n1. Introduction\n\nLet G be a cyclic group of order v. A subset D of size k of G is a cyclic diﬀerence\n\nset with parameter (v, k, λ)if for every non-identity element g of G the number of (x, y)\n\nsolutions of the equation g=xy−1 with x, y ∈ D is exactly λ.\n\nDiﬀerence sets have many applications in the construction of combinatorial designs,\n\nerror correcting codes, sequences with good correlation properties as well as the study of\n\nﬁnite geometries and cryptographic functions.\n\nAs it is the case with the construction of many other combinatorial objects, the theory\n\nof ﬁnite ﬁelds is of great importance in the construction of diﬀerence sets. Cyclic diﬀerence\n\nsets of special interest which are constructed from ﬁnite ﬁelds are the so called diﬀerence\n\nsets with Singer parameters for which either\n\nv=q n−1\n\nq−1, k =q n−1−1\n\nq−1, λ =q n−2−1\n\nq−1\n\nor\n\nv=q n−1\n\nq−1, k =q n−1, λ =q n−2(q−1)\n\nwhere q is a prime power and n≥3.\n\nIn this paper, we are interested in the construction of cyclic diﬀerence sets and linear\n\ncodes from polynomials and rational functions deﬁned over the binary ﬁnite ﬁeld F 2 m with\n\n2 m elements where m is an odd number. Our main result is the following theorem stated\n\nas Conjecture 36 in [5].\n\nTheorem 1. Let m≥5 be an odd number, F 2 m denote the degree-m extension of the\n\nbinary ﬁeld F 2, and F 2 m[x]denote the univariate polynomial ring over F 2 m. Furthermore,\n\nlet F∗\n\n2 m denote the multiplicative subgroup of F 2 m, and for a given f∈F 2 m[x],D(f)∗\n\ndenotes the punctured value-set it, that is,\n\nThis naturally leads to the partial resolution of another conjecture of Cun Sheng\n\nDing stated as Conjecture 37 [5] and appearing as Theorem 37 in this paper.\n\nThis paper is organized as follows. We end this section with a list on conventions and\n\nnotations. In Section 2, we gather some preliminary results. In Section 3, we show that the\n\nproof of the main result follows if we prove that the punctured value-set of four rational\n\nfunctions give rise to diﬀerence set with Singer parameters. In Section 4 we prove the\n\nmain result. Section 5 contains our partial resolution of a conjecture of Cun Sheng Ding\n\nabout tri-weight linear codes constructed from the above polynomials. Finally, Section 6\n\ncontains our concluding remarks.\n\n1.1. Conventions and Notations. In this subsection we\n\n•Throughout the paper m will be an odd integer, and σ= 2 m+1\n\n2.\n\n•F q,F q∗, and F q denote the ﬁnite ﬁeld with q elements, the multiplicative subgroup\n\nof F q, and the algebraic closure of F q, respectively.\n\n•For x∈F 2 m,Tr(x)denotes the absolute trace from F 2 m to F 2, that is,\n\nTr(x) = x+x 2+x 2 2+···+x 2 m−1.\n\n•We denote by T n the set of elements of F 2 m whose n-th power is of trace one, that\n\nis,\n\nT n={x∈GF (2 m)|Tr(x n) = 1},\n\n•We denote the set of elements of trace zero by T 0.\n\n•If X and Y are two sets and f:X−→ Y is a function, then for each y∈Y, the set\n\nof elements whose image under f is denoted by P f(y), that is,\n\nP f(y) = {x∈X|f(x) = y}.\n\n•If X and Y are two sets, f:X−→ Y is a function and for each y∈Y,|P f(y)| ∈\n\n{0,1, a}for some positive integer a, then f is called a (0,1, a)-map. In this paper,\n\na will be either 3 or 4.\n\n•For a set S,|S|denotes the number of elements of S.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDivisorial gonality of graphs, the slice rank polynomial method, and tensor products of convex cones | TU Delft Repository (https://repository.tudelft.nl/record/uuid%3Abb2db244-e032-46bd-a9d7-a36b9ce0ce0e)\nciteturn3search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.6 years ago; Crawled: last month; Doctoral Thesis (2023) ... Finite graph Metric graph Gonality Chip-firing game Treewidth Tree decomposition Monotone search strategy Slice rank Finite field System of balanced linear equations Convex cone Partially ordered vector space Ordered tensor product Face Extremal ray Order ideal ... Introduced by Baker around 2007, divisorial gonality links chip-firing games on graphs to classical algebraic geometry, including the combinatorial Riemann–Roch theorem and Brill–Noether theory.\n\ncontent_copy\n\nPublication Year\n\n2023\n\nLanguage\n\nEnglish\n\nDefense Date\n\n27-03-2023\n\nAwarding Institution\n\nDelft University of Technology\n\nISBN (print)\n\n978-94-6384-425-3\n\nDownloads counter\n\n555\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nThis dissertation explores three interconnected topics at the interface of combinatorics, algebra, and geometry.\nIn Part I, we study the divisorial gonality of graphs, a graph parameter inspired by algebraic geometry. Introduced by Baker around 2007, divisorial gonality links chip-firing games on graphs to classical algebraic geometry, including the combinatorial Riemann–Roch theorem and Brill–Noether theory. We contribute in two ways. First, we provide a constructive proof that treewidth is a lower bound for divisorial gonality, presenting a polynomial-time algorithm that transforms a positive rank effective divisor into a tree decomposition of bounded width. This bridges gonality with structural graph theory and enables dynamic programming techniques for graphs of bounded gonality. Second, we examine the Brill–Noether conjecture for graphs and disprove Baker’s subdivision conjecture by constructing graphs whose gonality exceeds that of the associated metric graphs. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGonality of metric graphs (https://boristheses.unibe.ch/3855/1/22vargas_aj.pdf)\nciteturn3search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Thus, the Brill-Noether theorem would be proven if for every genus $g$ ... See the intro of [GH80] and [HM06, Chapter 5] for details, including more on the history.8.1.3\\quad Gonality of a curveNow we look at an invariant called gonality [Amo93], to explain the difficulty of writing down a\nCHAPTER 8.  INTRODUCTION TO PART II64due to Griffiths and Harris [GH80], establishes that the expected dimension count is correct for  \nalmost all curves.Theorem 8.1.  There is a dense open subset \\(\\mathcal{B}\\mathcal{N}\\subset\\mathcal{M}_g\\) called the Brill-Noether general locus,\nsuch that for every \\(X\\in\\mathcal{B}\\mathcal{N}\\) we have:• (existence part) If \\(\\rho \\ge 0\\), then \\(\\dim G^r_d(X) = \\rho\\).\n\n• (non-existence part) If \\(\\rho < 0\\), then \\(G^r_d(X)\\) is empty.The curve $X$ is Brill-Noether general if $X$ is in $\\mathcal{BN}$; in this case, other facts are known about the geometry of $G^r_d(X)$. If $\\rho>0$, then $G^r_d(X)$ is an irreducible smooth scheme [Gie82; FL81]. If $\\rho=0$, then by [Kem71; KL72] the number of points in $G^r_d(X)$ is\\#G^r_d(X) = g! \\prod